<span style=3D"word-break: break-word; font-size: 18px;"><strong>How would =
it work?&nbsp;</strong>One of the plans would be to reverse a cut in the ba=
nk-profits surcharge, paid on top of corporation tax, in a move that would =
raise about =C2=A32bn a year. The surcharge had been introduced in 2016 at =
8 per cent but in 2023 was reduced to 3 per cent. The chancellor could also=
 introduce a one-off windfall tax but there have been warnings that interna=
tional banks with operations in Britain will shift investment overseas if t=
heir tax rates go up.&nbsp;</span>
</p><p dir=3D"ltr" style=3D"margin:0;font-size:18px;mso-line-height-alt:21p=
x">&nbsp;</p><p dir=3D"ltr" style=3D"margin:0;font-size:18px;mso-line-heigh=
t-alt:27px">
<span style=3D"word-break: break-word; font-size: 18px;"><strong>What else =
to expect: </strong>Economists have said that the government=E2=80=99s fisc=
al headroom has halved from =C2=A324bn. Healey is expected to allow the hea=
droom to fall to about =C2=A315bn to avoid much larger tax rises but still =
needs to find money to pay for what is likely to be a limited cost-of-livin=
g bailout.&nbsp;</span></p><p dir=3D"ltr" style=3D"margin:0;font-size:18px;=
mso-line-height-alt:21px">&nbsp;</p><p dir=3D"ltr"=20
